## 🧠 PART 3 — Background Script Logic (`background.js`)

---

### 🎯 GOAL
The **background service worker** is responsible for:

1. Receiving code snippets from the content script.  
2. Sending them to your **free code analysis API (Hugging Face)**.  
3. Handling rate limits / network errors gracefully.  
4. Returning a **structured, summarized feedback** string back to the content script.  
5. Optionally — run local linting when offline or when the API fails.  

---

## ⚙️ WHY WE NEED A BACKGROUND SCRIPT

Chrome doesn’t allow **content scripts** to directly call external APIs (like Hugging Face)  
for two reasons:
- **Security (CORS restrictions):** content scripts are sandboxed in webpage context.  
- **Privacy:** prevents websites from sniffing API keys or traffic.  

So, the **background script** acts as a **secure proxy**.  
It’s always active (in Manifest V3 it runs as a *service worker* — wakes up when needed, then sleeps).

---

## 🧩 FLOW DIAGRAM

```
Content.js → "Here's some code text."
    ↓
Background.js → sends to Hugging Face API
    ↓
Gets AI feedback or fallback ESLint results
    ↓
Sends back to content.js for display
```

---

## 🧩 STEP-BY-STEP IMPLEMENTATION

### 1️⃣ Message Listener Setup

Your background script listens for incoming messages from content.js.

```js
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(async (msg, sender) => {
  if (msg.action === "analyze_code") {
    console.log("🔍 Received code for analysis");

    const feedback = await analyzeCode(msg.code);

    // Send response back to the same tab
    chrome.tabs.sendMessage(sender.tab.id, {
      action: "inject_feedback",
      id: msg.id,
      feedback
    });
  }
});
```

🧠 **Logic Breakdown:**
- `onMessage.addListener` = sets up a listener for all extension messages.  
- `msg.action` lets you differentiate between types of events (we only care about `"analyze_code"`).  
- `sender.tab.id` gives you the tab that sent the message — so you can reply specifically to that tab.  

---

### 2️⃣ The Core Function: `analyzeCode()`

We’ll use the **Hugging Face free inference API**.  
It allows up to ~30–50 requests/hour, no payment needed.

```js
async function analyzeCode(code) {
  const token = "hf_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"; // your Hugging Face key
  const url = "https://api-inference.huggingface.co/models/Salesforce/codet5-base";

  const prompt = `You are a senior software engineer reviewing code. 
  Review the following code, point out logic issues, inefficiencies, 
  and give concise improvement tips (3-5 bullet points max):\n\n${code}`;

  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: {
        "Authorization": `Bearer ${token}`,
        "Content-Type": "application/json"
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ inputs: prompt })
    });

    if (!res.ok) {
      throw new Error(`API returned ${res.status}`);
    }

    const data = await res.json();
    const text = data?.[0]?.generated_text || "No feedback available.";

    return formatFeedback(text);

  } catch (err) {
    console.warn("⚠️ API failed, running offline lint instead:", err.message);
    return runOfflineLint(code);
  }
}
```

---

### 🧠 Why Each Piece Exists

| Code Section | Why It’s Needed | What It Solves |
|---------------|----------------|----------------|
| `const token` | Authenticates your Hugging Face request. | Required by API. |
| `fetch(url, {...})` | Standard HTTP call. | Sends code for analysis. |
| `if (!res.ok)` | Detects API errors early. | Avoids cryptic runtime errors. |
| `data?.[0]?.generated_text` | Some models return array of outputs. | Ensures safe parsing. |
| `catch { runOfflineLint(code) }` | Backup system for free. | Keeps extension functional offline. |

---

### 3️⃣ Formatting the AI Feedback

The API response is usually a raw string,  
so we make it **consistent and readable** before injecting it into the page.

```js
function formatFeedback(rawText) {
  const points = rawText.split(/[\r\n•-]/)
    .map(line => line.trim())
    .filter(line => line.length > 0)
    .slice(0, 6);

  return points.map(p => "• " + p).join("\n");
}
```

💡 **Why Format It?**
- Makes feedback structured.  
- Easy to read inside the sidebar or tooltip.  
- Ensures consistent UI layout even if the model rambles.

---

### 4️⃣ Offline Fallback — Local Linting

We don’t want your extension to break if the user loses internet or API limit hits.  

So we’ll run **simple static analysis** using regex or local rules:  

```js
function runOfflineLint(code) {
  let feedback = [];

  if (code.includes("==") && !code.includes("===")) {
    feedback.push("Use '===' instead of '==' for strict comparison.");
  }
  if (code.match(/for\s*\(.*\)\s*{[\s\S]*for\s*\(/)) {
    feedback.push("Nested loops detected – may cause performance issues.");
  }
  if (code.length > 800) {
    feedback.push("Consider modularizing – long functions reduce readability.");
  }
  if (!/function|class/.test(code)) {
    feedback.push("Add functions or classes for better structure.");
  }

  return feedback.length > 0
    ? feedback.map(p => "• " + p).join("\n")
    : "✅ No major issues found.";
}
```

🧠 **Why Offline Linting Matters**
- Guarantees reliability (no “dead” UI).  
- Keeps user trust (extension always responds).  
- Adds a technical edge for your resume (“hybrid online-offline analysis”).

---

### 5️⃣ Final Data Flow Recap

| Step | From → To | Data Sent |
|------|------------|-----------|
| 1 | `content.js` → `background.js` | `{ id, code }` |
| 2 | `background.js` → Hugging Face | `prompt` (string) |
| 3 | `background.js` → `content.js` | `{ id, feedback }` |
| 4 | `content.js` → webpage | Injected tooltip & sidebar |

---

### 🔒 Security & Privacy Notes
- The API key is stored **inside background.js** — *never* inside content.js.  
  → content.js runs inside user pages; background.js does not.  
- Chrome isolates service workers; API key won’t leak.  
- You can further protect it using a simple obfuscation (`btoa()` encode).

